Practical 5: Workflow Customization in Jira
Objective

A. Design a custom workflow.
B. Add transitions and conditions.
C. Publish the workflow and associate it with your project.

We will use your existing Syllogix (SYL) Kanban project and follow the current Jira Cloud layout.

A workflow defines the statuses an issue passes through and the transitions users can use to move it between statuses.
A. Design a custom workflow
Step 1: Open Workflow settings
1. Click the ⚙️ Settings icon at the top-right of Jira.
2. Select Work items.
3. Under Work items, open Workflows.
4. Click Add workflow.
5. Select the option to create a new workflow, if Jira asks.
6. Enter:
- Name: Student Management Workflow
- Description: Workflow for managing student management project tasks.
7. Click Add or Create, whichever appears.

If you cannot find Add workflow, you may not have permission to create workflows. Ask your Jira administrator for workflow editing permission. 
Atlassian Support+1

Step 2: Add statuses
In the workflow editor, create or add these statuses:
Status	Purpose
To Do	Work has not started
In Progress	Work is being done
In Review	Work is being checked
Done	Work is completed

1. Click Add status in the workflow editor.
2. Add In Review if it does not already exist.
3. Use the existing To Do, In Progress, and Done statuses if available.
4. Confirm that all four statuses appear in the workflow diagram.
Do not create duplicate statuses if the required ones already exist.
Step 3: Arrange the workflow
Your workflow should follow this sequence:
To Do

In Progress

In Review

Done


This is the workflow you will configure in the next section.
B. Add transitions and conditions
Step 4: Add transitions
A transition controls how an issue moves from one status to another.

In the workflow editor:
1. Click Add transition.
2. Set From status to To Do.
3. Set To status to In Progress.
4. Enter the transition name Start Progress.
5. Click Add.
Repeat these steps for the following transitions:
From status	To status	Transition name
To Do	In Progress	Start Progress
In Progress	In Review	Submit for Review
In Review	In Progress	Request Changes
In Review	Done	Approve and Complete

If the editor lets you create a transition by dragging between status boxes, you can use that method instead.

Remember: transitions are directional. To move from In Review back to In Progress, you need a separate transition. 
Atlassian Support

Step 5: Add a condition
For this practical, configure the Approve and Complete transition so that only the assigned user can move an issue to Done.
1. In the workflow diagram, click the transition arrow named Approve and Complete.
2. Open Rules in the details panel.
3. Find Restrict transition or the option to add a restriction.
4. Select the restriction that limits who can move a work item.
5. Choose Only assignee if that option is available.
6. Save or apply the rule.
If your editor shows different condition names, send me a screenshot before selecting anything.

Note: This condition restricts the transition. If the issue is unassigned, users may not be able to complete it until it is assigned to someone.
C. Publish and associate the workflow with your project
Step 6: Publish the workflow
1. Review the workflow diagram and transitions.
2. Click Update workflow, Publish, or the equivalent option shown in your editor.
3. If Jira asks whether to save a copy of the existing workflow, choose to save a copy if you want a backup.
4. Confirm the publishing action.
The new workflow must be published before you can use it in your project. The exact button depends on the editor version. 
Atlassian Support+1

Step 7: Associate the workflow with Syllogix
1. Open your Syllogix project.
2. Click the ••• next to the project name, or open Space settings.
3. Find Workflows in the settings sidebar.
4. Click Add workflow or Edit workflow, depending on what appears.
5. If prompted, select Add existing workflow.
6. Select Student Management Workflow.
7. Select the work types that should use it. For this practical, select Story, Task, and Bug.
8. Click Next, then Finish if those buttons appear.
9. Click Publish or Publish and associate.
10. If Jira asks you to map existing statuses to the new workflow, map them to the corresponding statuses and confirm.
If your settings page does not show these options, send me a screenshot of the Workflows page. Do not change the existing workflow scheme without checking which project it affects.
